# --- @fill: 3rd-person verb forms (root-changing) ----------------------------
# The deck's Part 2 drills 3rd-person singular with De Pol as subject. The
# root-changing forms (baakt, liest, fiert, mécht) are the target; distractors
# are wrong persons of the same verb.

@fill
@lu Mäi Partner [baakt] gär Kuchen.
@en My partner likes [baking] cakes.
@distractor-lu baken
@distractor-lu bake
@distractor-lu gebacken
@distractor-en cooking
@distractor-en drawing
@distractor-en singing

@fill
@lu Mäi Partner [liest] gär Bicher.
@en My partner likes [reading] books.
@distractor-lu liesen
@distractor-lu lies
@distractor-lu gelies
@distractor-en cooking
@distractor-en singing
@distractor-en swimming

@fill
@lu Mäi Partner [fiert] ganz gär Vëlo.
@en My partner really likes [riding] the bike.
@distractor-lu fueren
@distractor-lu fuere
@distractor-lu gefuer
@distractor-en swimming
@distractor-en running
@distractor-en cooking

@fill
@lu Mäi Partner [mécht] ganz gär Fotoen.
@en My partner really likes [taking] photos.
@distractor-lu maachen
@distractor-lu maache
@distractor-lu gemaach
@distractor-en collecting
@distractor-en baking
@distractor-en singing

# --- @fill: gär liking scale --------------------------------------------------
# gär / net gär / guer net gär / ganz gär — the four-step scale from Part 2.
# Distractors are the other points on the scale.

@fill
@lu Ech mole [gär] mat menge Kanner.
@en I [like] drawing with my children.
@distractor-lu net gär
@distractor-lu guer net gär
@distractor-lu seelen
@distractor-en don't like
@distractor-en really don't like
@distractor-en rarely like

@fill
@lu Ech schwamme [net gär] moies.
@en I [don't like] swimming in the morning.
@distractor-lu gär
@distractor-lu ganz gär
@distractor-lu ëmmer
@distractor-en like
@distractor-en really like
@distractor-en always like

@fill
@lu Mäi Partner sammelt [guer net gär] Timberen.
@en My partner [really doesn't like] collecting stamps.
@distractor-lu gär
@distractor-lu ganz gär
@distractor-lu net gär
@distractor-en likes
@distractor-en really likes
@distractor-en doesn't like

@fill
@lu Ech fuere [ganz gär] Vëlo.
@en I [really like] riding the bike.
@distractor-lu net gär
@distractor-lu guer net gär
@distractor-lu seelen
@distractor-en don't like
@distractor-en really don't like
@distractor-en rarely like

# --- @fill: frequency adverbs -------------------------------------------------
# ëmmer / seelen plus the "X Mol d'Woch" and "all Dag" patterns from the deck.
# Distractors are other frequency adverbs to force the learner to read meaning.

@fill
@lu Ech lauschtere [ëmmer] Musek.
@en I [always] listen to music.
@distractor-lu heiansdo
@distractor-lu seelen
@distractor-lu ni
@distractor-en sometimes
@distractor-en rarely
@distractor-en never

@fill
@lu Ech kache [seelen] de Weekend.
@en I [rarely] cook at the weekend.
@distractor-lu dacks
@distractor-lu ëmmer
@distractor-lu heiansdo
@distractor-en often
@distractor-en always
@distractor-en sometimes

@fill
@lu Ech gi [dräi Mol] d'Woch lafen.
@en I go running [three times] a week.
@distractor-lu ee Mol
@distractor-lu zwee Mol
@distractor-lu all Dag
@distractor-en once
@distractor-en twice
@distractor-en every day

@fill
@lu Ech gi [all Dag] schwammen.
@en I go swimming [every day].
@distractor-lu ee Mol d'Woch
@distractor-lu heiansdo
@distractor-lu seelen
@distractor-en once a week
@distractor-en sometimes
@distractor-en rarely

# --- @fill: separable verbs ---------------------------------------------------
# opstoen (stinn … op) and the goen + infinitive pattern (schwamme goen,
# spadséiere goen) where the non-conjugated verb goes to the end. The deck
# explicitly teaches these as separable-verb-like constructions.

@fill
@lu De Samschdeg stinn ech um zéng Auer [op].
@en On Saturday I get [up] at ten o'clock.
@distractor-lu aus
@distractor-lu un
@distractor-lu mat
@distractor-en out
@distractor-en on
@distractor-en off

@fill
@lu De Weekend gi meng Kanner [spadséieren].
@en At the weekend my children go for a [walk].
@distractor-lu schwammen
@distractor-lu lafen
@distractor-lu sangen
@distractor-en swim
@distractor-en run
@distractor-en sing

@fill
@lu Ech gi freides owes [schwammen].
@en I go [swimming] on Friday evenings.
@distractor-lu lafen
@distractor-lu spadséieren
@distractor-lu kachen
@distractor-en running
@distractor-en walking
@distractor-en cooking

# --- @fill: prepositions of time -----------------------------------------------
# um (at a clock time), vun (from), nom (after, contracted), zu Mëtteg (at noon).
# Distractors are other time prepositions to test which one fits.

@fill
@lu Ech spille [um] zéng Auer Piano.
@en I play the piano [at] ten o'clock.
@distractor-lu vun
@distractor-lu nom
@distractor-lu bis
@distractor-en from
@distractor-en after
@distractor-en until

@fill
@lu Ech gi freides [vun] siwen bis aacht Auer lafen.
@en I go running on Fridays [from] seven to eight o'clock.
@distractor-lu um
@distractor-lu nom
@distractor-lu zu
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en after
@distractor-en to

@fill
@lu [Nom] Mëttegiesse ginn ech spadséieren.
@en [After] lunch I go for a walk.
@distractor-lu Um
@distractor-lu Vun
@distractor-lu Zu
@distractor-en At
@distractor-en From
@distractor-en To

@fill
@lu Ech gi [zu] Mëtteg a mäi Liiblingsrestaurant.
@en I go to my favourite restaurant [at] noon.
@distractor-lu um
@distractor-lu vun
@distractor-lu nom
@distractor-en from
@distractor-en after
@distractor-en by
